{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The integration claim is broader than its refutation condition. The relation asserts the state record incorporates BOTH 'the machine's repertoire exhaustion AND refuted attractors' from the basin report, but the refutation condition tests only whether 'the state record does not include the basin report's repertoire exhaustion findings.' If the state record included repertoire exhaustion but omitted the refuted attractors, the integration would be incomplete—yet the stated refutation condition would not fire, because it never checks for the attractors. A refuted-if clause that fails to cover a component explicitly named in the relation claim is a substantive gap: the condition under-filters relative to the asserted relation.","target":"c19db685345d1513a6c141614320d532e88c2c4d633fe8978181cb4dd8c0a2e8"},{"attack":true,"case":"The relation is internally contradictory in kind. The target names a reduction ('reduces to') and then glosses it as 'a specialization or direct extension.' A 'specialization' is consistent with reduction (everything in the specialization is derivable from the general case). But a 'direct extension' by definition adds content beyond what it extends—exactly what the refutation condition proscribes ('refuted if it introduces claims... not derivable from the documented state'). So one of the two disjuncts the target offers as its relation description ('direct extension') is self-refuting under its own refuted-if clause. The target cannot coherently describe the same relation as both a reduction-to and a direct-extension-of the same artifact.","target":"fc49d1171b2cb5e161e457923ce2cec578f60c8682f5d646ced1dbda2e6d5312"}]}